{
  $schema: "https://docs.renovatebot.com/renovate-schema.json",
  // Policy: minor, patch and digest updates automerge. Majors always wait for a
  // human. packageRules are applied in order and later rules win, so the
  // exceptions at the bottom override the blanket rules at the top.

  // Merging is GitHub's job, not Renovate's: Renovate sets auto-merge on the PR
  // and GitHub merges it the moment the required checks go green. That decouples
  // merging from Renovate's run schedule, which is the whole point.
  //
  // This was `false` until 2026-09-21, on the stated premise that branch
  // protection is "not available for a private repo on this plan". The repo is
  // public, so the premise was simply wrong. What it cost: PR #15, an OpenTofu
  // lockfile update, could not merge for nine days. Renovate's terraform manager
  // regenerates .terraform.lock.hcl on every run and reports it as updated
  // without diffing it against the branch, so `updatedArtifacts` is never empty,
  // and that forces `reuseExistingBranch: false` ("Existing branch needs
  // updating. Restarting processBranch() with a clean branch") no matter what
  // rebaseWhen says. So the branch was force-pushed with a byte-identical lock
  // file -- same blob sha across all eleven pushes -- validate-manifests
  // restarted, and seven seconds later the same run read the status as pending
  // and skipped the merge. Every run, forever. With GitHub holding the PR the
  // rebase churn stops deciding anything.
  //
  // Needs `allow_auto_merge` on the repo AND a rule requiring the checks, or
  // GitHub rejects the auto-merge request and Renovate falls back to merging
  // here. Both are in place: ruleset "main-required-checks" on main requires
  // kustomize build, helm template and GitGuardian Security Checks. It leaves
  // "require branches to be up to date" OFF on purpose -- turning it on would
  // stall every PR behind a rebase, which is the failure above all over again.
  platformAutomerge: true,

  // The default `rebaseWhen: auto` resolves to `behind-base-branch` whenever
  // automerge is enabled, and that capped this repo at ONE automerge per run.
  // Observed in the 2026-09-14 run: Renovate merged cert-manager, logged
  // "Restarting repository job after automerge result", and the restart found
  // every other automerge branch a commit behind main -- so it rebased all six.
  // Each force-push restarts validate-manifests, so those checks were pending at
  // the moment that same run read them, and nothing else could merge. Repeat
  // daily: one PR merges, the rest get pushed back to pending. Nine PRs had
  // queued up behind that, every one of them green.
  //
  // With `conflicted` the branches hold still, keep their green checks, and the
  // restart after an automerge can take the next eligible PR immediately. That is
  // TWO automerges per run, not a drained queue: Renovate restarts the repository
  // job exactly once, `renovateRepository(repoConfig, false)` in
  // workers/repository/index.ts, and the second pass logs "Automerged but already
  // retried once" rather than restarting again. Not configurable. Verified on the
  // 2026-09-14 push run, which merged secrets-operator and helm and stopped.
  // Clearing a backlog therefore takes ceil(n/2) runs -- extra `workflow_dispatch`
  // runs are the lever, and they are cheap now that no branch gets rebased.
  //
  // It also stops ~16 pointless validate-manifests jobs per run.
  //
  // The cost, which Renovate's docs state plainly: two updates can merge one
  // after another without having been tested together, and a PR's checks may have
  // run against an older main. That is the same reconcile-time bet the rest of
  // this file already makes -- `wait: true` plus healthChecks catch what
  // merge-time cannot. The docs' other objection (automerge stalls once a PR is
  // out of date) applies only where the branch rule requires up-to-date PRs,
  // which the main ruleset deliberately does not -- see platformAutomerge above.
  rebaseWhen: "conflicted",

  packageRules: [
    {
      // Applies to every manager and datasource in this repo -- container
      // images, Helm/OCI charts, GitHub Actions, mise tools, OpenTofu
      // providers. The safety net is reconcile-time, not merge-time: every app
      // Kustomization runs `wait: true` with a healthCheck, so an image or
      // chart that fails its probes leaves the Kustomization NotReady instead
      // of quietly breaking. `flux get kustomizations` is where that shows up.
      //
      // automergeType: pr -- every update gets a real PR, which Renovate merges
      // once its checks pass. It was `branch` until 2026-09-08, where Renovate
      // merged the branch into main and opened no PR at all. Two things made that
      // untenable: GitGuardian only posts its check on a PR (verified -- present
      // on a PR head, absent on a push to main), so secret scanning never gated
      // an automerged update; and an update that merges with no PR leaves nothing
      // to read afterwards. The cost is PR noise for updates that merge minutes
      // later, which is the cheaper side of the trade.
      //
      // Tests are deliberately NOT ignored (no ignoreTests here): the
      // validate-manifests workflow runs on pull_request AND on `renovate/**`
      // pushes, with no paths filter, so a check always exists for Renovate to
      // wait on. Two consequences:
      //
      //   1. This depends on the GitHub App holding "Commit statuses" read --
      //      the permission that was missing from 2026-09-03 to 09-08 and made
      //      every run silently do nothing. If automerge stops happening, check
      //      that first.
      //   2. Renovate no longer merges these itself -- it hands the PR to
      //      GitHub's auto-merge (see platformAutomerge at the top of this
      //      file), so a PR merges minutes after its checks go green rather
      //      than on the next Renovate run. Before 2026-09-21 the check was
      //      read during a run, so a newly opened PR could lag up to a day,
      //      and until 2026-09-14 the default rebaseWhen re-pended every
      //      queued branch on every run, so only one PR could merge per run
      //      regardless of how many were green. See the rebaseWhen note above.
      description: "Automerge minor, patch and digest updates",
      matchUpdateTypes: ["minor", "patch", "digest"],
      automerge: true,
      automergeType: "pr",
    },
    {
      // Explicit rather than implied. Renovate defaults to automerge: false, so
      // this changes nothing today -- it exists so that a future rule (or an
      // upstream change in the home-operations preset) cannot quietly enable
      // automerge for a major. Majors get read, reasoned about, and merged by
      // hand.
      //
      // This covers semver majors only. A 0.x dep's breaking boundary is its
      // minor, and Renovate types that as "minor" -- see the next rule.
      description: "Never automerge majors -- manual verification and merge",
      matchUpdateTypes: ["major"],
      automerge: false,
    },
    {
      // For a 0.x dep the breaking boundary is the MINOR: Renovate types
      // 0.7.1 -> 0.8.0 as a minor update, so the blanket rule above would
      // automerge it, while the preset's own commit message calls the same bump
      // breaking -- `feat(container)!`, which is how plugin-barman-cloud 0.8.0
      // arrived on 2026-09-08. Treat those like majors: read them, merge by hand.
      // matchCurrentVersion takes a regex when wrapped in slashes.
      description: "0.x deps: never automerge minors -- for 0.x, minor is breaking",
      matchCurrentVersion: "/^0\\./",
      matchUpdateTypes: ["minor"],
      automerge: false,
    },
    {
      // Release-age cooldown, kept separate from the automerge decision so it
      // applies to majors too. A yanked Action release is caught before we
      // adopt it.
      description: "GitHub Actions: 3-day release-age cooldown",
      matchManagers: ["github-actions"],
      minimumReleaseAge: "3 days",
    },
    {
      // kubectl is the one dep where Renovate's "minor" is not a safe category:
      // it types 1.35.8 -> 1.37.0 as minor because the major is 1, but that is
      // two Kubernetes minors and outside the supported +/-1 client-to-server
      // skew. allowedVersions.json5 constrains it to ~1.35; this rule exists so
      // that constraint is not the ONLY thing standing between a bad kubectl
      // and main, because it silently failed to match once already (2026-09-08).
      // Bump kubectl by hand, together with the k3s cluster.
      //
      // Must stay last: later rules win.
      description: "kubectl: never automerge (review against the k3s server minor)",
      matchManagers: ["mise"],
      matchDepNames: ["kubectl"],
      automerge: false,
      // Majors and 0.x-breaking-minors above already read as "needs a human"
      // because Renovate's own isBreaking flag puts a "!" in their commit
      // type (e.g. "feat(container)!:"). kubectl's updates aren't breaking by
      // that definition -- they're excluded above for a different reason --
      // so nothing marks them without this override. Force the same "!" onto
      // the "chore(mise):" prefix so a kubectl PR reads as manual-merge in
      // the PR list title without opening it.
      commitMessagePrefix: "chore(mise)!:",
    },
  ],
}
